  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 PM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nA few light showers are developing across the Brush Country this \nafternoon. This activity is expected to increase in areal coverage \nthis evening with widespread showers and thunderstorms developing \novernight. Abundant moisture combined with an embedded short wave \nout ahead of an approaching long wave trough, and strengthening low \nlevel moisture convergence overnight, will be conducive for heavy \nrainfall. The low level convergence/surface low axis, is expected to \nbe located initially across the Brush country overnight with the \naxis shifting east through Sunday morning. There is concern that the \nsurface low will move slowly allowing for training over some areas \nresulting in flooding and flash flooding. Confidence is high for \nwidespread convection, but there remains some uncertainty with \ntiming and location of the surface low, which makes it tricky to \nknow just where the highest bullseye of rainfall may occur. Models \nare indicating another round of showers and storms Sunday night into \nMonday across S TX. Overall, rainfall totals for this event are \nexpected to be 1-3 inches across the Victoria Crossroads and Coastal \nBend, up to 2-4 inches across the Brush Country. Isolated 7-9 inches \nis possible. Most of S TX is in a Flood Watch through Monday \nmorning. Currently the Victoria Crossroads is not included in the \nwatch, but may be included with later updates depending on where the \nheaviest rainfall sets up. \n\nAnother concern is high risk of rip currents and minor coastal \nflooding Sunday. The chance of minor coastal flooding along gulf \nfacing beaches will increase on Sunday during high tide. This is due \nto a strengthening east-northeast flow across the gulf waters \ntonight into Sunday, which is conducive for pushing the water up to \nthe dunes during high tide. In addition, normal tide levels will \nalso be on the increase as we head toward a new moon. Latest P-ETSS \nrun indicated that tide levels may exceed the 2ft MSL threshold \nduring high tide Sunday and Monday with with up to 2.5 feet above \nMSL possible. The caveat is that models keep the long period swells \nat 7 seconds or less, but this may be under-estimated. Will continue \nto monitor trends for any changes to the tide levels and swells.",
